The '''2015 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match played between [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] and [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] at [[Wembley Stadium]], London, on 30 May 2015. Organised by [[the Football Association]] (FA), it was the [[List of FA Cup finals|134th final]] of the [[Football Association Challenge Cup]] (FA Cup), the world's oldest football cup competition.  En route to the final, Arsenal defeated [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]], [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]], [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]], [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] and [[Reading F.C.|Reading]] (after [[extra time]]).  Aston Villa secured victories over [[Blackpool F.C.|Blackpool]], [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]], [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]], [[West Bromwich Albion F.C.|West Bromwich Albion]] and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in the rounds before the final.

[[Theo Walcott]] gave Arsenal the lead in the 40th minute. [[Nacho Monreal]] [[Cross (association football)|crossed]] from the left side of the pitch to [[Alexis Sánchez]] whose [[Header (association football)|header]] found Walcott who struck the ball on the [[Volley (association football)|volley]] into the goal.  Ten minutes later, Alexis Sánchez struck the ball from around {{convert|30|yd}} into the roof of the Aston Villa net to double his side's lead before [[Per Mertesacker]] made it 3&ndash;0 with a header midway through the second half.  Three minutes into [[stoppage time]], [[Olivier Giroud]] scored from close range to give Arsenal a 4&ndash;0 victory.  It was the sixth FA Cup win for manager [[Arsène Wenger]], putting him in joint first place for wins with [[George Ramsay (footballer, born 1855)|George Ramsay]].

Arsenal had already qualified for the [[2015–16 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] by finishing third in the [[2014–15 Premier League|Premier League]], and following a change in [[UEFA]] rules, the remaining [[2015–16 Europa League|Europa League]] place went to [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]], the highest-placed Premier League team that had not qualified for any European competition instead of Aston Villa.  As FA Cup winners, Arsenal played [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], winners of the Premier League, in the [[2015 FA Community Shield]] at Wembley.

==Background==
The [[FA Cup]] is an annual [[knockout tournament]] involving professional and amateur men's [[association football|football]] clubs in the [[English football league system]].{{sfn|O'Leary|2017|p=63}} It is the world's oldest football cup competition.<ref name=finals>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals, 1872 – today |access-date=9 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170613182709/http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |archive-date=13 June 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ArsenalManUtdChelseaLiverpool">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |access-date=5 October 2020 |title=Who has won the most FA Cups? The 12 most successful clubs in the world's oldest competition |first=Sam |last=Blitz |date=3 August 2020 |archive-date=5 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005103153/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |url-status=live }}</ref> The 2015 final was the [[List of FA Cup Finals|134th final]] to be played since it was first held in 1872.<ref name=finals/>

[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] were playing a record nineteenth FA Cup final, surpassing [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]].  Of these, they had won eleven, a joint record with Manchester United, most recently the previous season's final against [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]]. Their most recent defeat in the final was [[2001 FA Cup final|in 2001]], losing 2–1 to [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]].<ref name=rsssf>{{cite web |title=England FA Challenge Cup Finals |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engcuphist.html |website=[[RSSSF]] |access-date=19 April 2015 | first= James M. | last = Ross| archive-date=13 January 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100113115049/http://rsssf.com/tablese/engcuphist.html |url-status=live }}</ref>  It was [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]]'s eleventh final, of which they had won seven, most recently [[1957 FA Cup final|in 1957]] with a 2–1 win over Manchester United. Their most recent final was a 1–0 defeat to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in 2000.<ref name=rsssf/>

Arsenal and Aston Villa played twice during [[2014–15 Premier League|the league season]], both games resulting in Arsenal victories. On 20 September 2014, they won 3–0 at [[Villa Park]] with three goals in less than four minutes of the first half, from [[Mesut Özil]], [[Danny Welbeck]] and an [[own goal]] from [[Aly Cissokho]] off a shot from [[Kieran Gibbs]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Mandeep |last=Sanghera |title=Aston Villa 0–3 Arsenal |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/29187273 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=20 September 2014 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=11 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150511003604/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/29187273 |url-status=live }}</ref> At their home ground, the [[Emirates Stadium]], on 1 February 2015, Arsenal triumphed 5–0 with goals by [[Olivier Giroud]], Özil, [[Theo Walcott]], a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] by [[Santi Cazorla]] after goalkeeper [[Brad Guzan]] had committed a [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)|foul]] on [[Chuba Akpom]], and [[Héctor Bellerín]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=Dawkes |title=Arsenal 5–0 Aston Villa |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30971833 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=1 February 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=7 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150407143449/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/30971833 |url-status=live }}</ref>

