Also a Premier League team, Aston Villa entered the tournament in the third round with a 1–0 home win over Championship club [[Blackpool F.C.|Blackpool]]. [[Christian Benteke]] scored the only goal two minutes from the end of the game when he controlled a pass from [[Carlos Sánchez (Colombian footballer)|Carlos Sánchez]] before striking it past Blackpool goalkeeper [[Joe Lewis (footballer, born 1987)|Joe Lewis]] from around {{convert|18|yd}}.<ref>{{cite web |first=Steve |last=Canavan |title=Aston Villa 1–0 Blackpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30614386 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=4 January 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=23 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150223125938/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/30614386 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the fourth round, they faced another second-tier team at home, [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]].  After a goalless first half, [[Carles Gil]] opened the scoring for Aston Villa on his debut six minutes after half-time with a {{convert|25|yd|adj=on}} curling strike past [[Lee Camp (footballer)|Lee Camp]].  Twenty minutes later, the lead was doubled by [[Andreas Weimann]] who scored from [[Alan Hutton]]'s pass.  Bournemouth substitute [[Callum Wilson (footballer, born 1992)|Callum Wilson]] scored in second-half stoppage time but Aston Villa won the match 2&ndash;1.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Aston Villa 2–1 Bournemouth |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/30868889 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 January 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=5 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150405194141/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/30868889 |url-status=live }}</ref>  Two weeks after the fourth round, Aston Villa's manager [[Paul Lambert]] was dismissed with his side in the [[Promotion and relegation|relegation]] positions in the Premier League.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/31431649 | title = Aston Villa: Paul Lambert sacked as manager | date = 12 February 2015 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 29 May 2021 | archive-date = 2 June 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210602215425/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/31431649 | url-status = live }}</ref>  He was replaced by [[Tim Sherwood]] and in the fifth round, his side were drawn at home, this time against Premier League club [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]].  The first half ended 0&ndash;0 but [[Leandro Bacuna]] scored midway through the second half with a curling shot from outside the Leicester City penalty area. [[Scott Sinclair]] scored Aston Villa's second in the 89th minute after the ball was fumbled by [[Mark Schwarzer]], the Leicester City goalkeeper.  [[Andrej Kramarić]] scored a consolation goal one minute into stoppage time but Aston Villa progressed with a 2&ndash;1 win.<ref>{{cite web |first=Patrick |last=Jennings |title=Aston Villa 2–1 Leicester |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/31423131 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 February 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=18 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150218024315/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/31423131 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The sixth round draw saw Aston Villa drawn at home against [[Aston Villa F.C.–West Bromwich Albion F.C. rivalry|West Midlands rivals]] [[West Bromwich Albion F.C.|West Bromwich Albion]].  After a goalless first half, [[Fabian Delph]] gave the home side the lead after receiving the ball from [[Charles N'Zogbia]] and striking it low past West Bromwich Albion goalkeeper [[Boaz Myhill]].  With ten minutes to go, West Bromwich Albion's [[Claudio Yacob]] was sent off after receiving a second yellow card and Sinclair doubled Aston Villa's lead five minutes later.  Substitute [[Jack Grealish]], who had been on the pitch less than 20 minutes, was sent off in second-half stoppage time for a second yellow card, but his side won 2&ndash;0.  The victory prompted a [[pitch invasion]] for which Aston Villa were charged by [[The Football Association]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Aston Villa 2–0 West Brom |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/31669806 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=7 March 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=9 March 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150309002006/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/31669806 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="FACharge">{{cite web |title=Aston Villa & Reading charged over FA Cup pitch invasions |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/32398274 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=21 April 2015 |access-date=21 April 2015 |archive-date=22 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150422100905/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/32398274 |url-status=live }}</ref>{{efn|In May 2015, Aston Villa were fined £200,000 following the sixth round pitch invasions and were severely warned as to the club's future conduct.<ref name="200k">{{cite web |title=Aston Villa fined £200,000 after FA Cup pitch invasions |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/32743437 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=14 May 2015 |access-date=14 May 2015 |archive-date=16 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150516004356/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/32743437 |url-status=live }}</ref>}} In the semi-final at Wembley Stadium, Aston Villa faced Liverpool.  [[Philippe Coutinho]] gave Liverpool the lead in the 30th minute, shooting past [[Shay Given]] in the Aston Villa goal.  Benteke scored the equaliser six minutes later, sidefooting the ball past [[Simon Mignolet]], the Liverpool goalkeeper.  Delph then gave Aston Villa the lead 11 minutes into the second half to secure his side a 2&ndash;1 victory.  Aston Villa reached the final for the first time [[2000 FA Cup final|since 2000]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Aston Villa 2–1 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/32273487 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 April 2015 |access-date=19 April 2015 |archive-date=20 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150420020333/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/32273487 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
[[File:"Watch the Gunnersaurus"! (16420684192).jpg|thumb|left|Arsenal defeated Aston Villa 5–0 in the Premier League on 1 February 2015. Pictured are Villa's [[Fabian Delph]] and  Arsenal's [[Per Mertesacker]], both club [[captain (association football)|captain]]s.]]
Children's television programme ''[[Blue Peter]]'' ran a competition to design the mascot for the final. The winning entry was "Billie", a horse inspired by the one which controlled the crowds ahead of [[1923 FA Cup final|the 1923 final]]. Former England international [[Phil Neville]], one of the judges in the competition, said that: "I think the design is great, the story behind it is even better and I think it’s something everyone’s going to be really impressed with.”<ref>{{cite web |title=Billie the White Horse wins the Blue Peter FA Mascot Competition |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/mediacentre/latestnews/2015/blue-peter-fa-mascot-competition |publisher=[[BBC]] |date=16 April 2015 |access-date=17 April 2015 |archive-date=23 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150623132410/http://www.bbc.co.uk/mediacentre/latestnews/2015/blue-peter-fa-mascot-competition |url-status=live }}</ref>  Religious television programme ''[[Songs of Praise]]'' ran a competition to be part of a 64-person choir to sing the traditional hymn "[[Abide with Me]]" before the final. The 64 were fans of each of the clubs which reached the third round of the competition, including a 99-year-old [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] fan and a Liverpool fan who survived the [[Hillsborough disaster]]. The music was performed by the [[Band of the Brigade of Gurkhas]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Songs of Praise FA Cup Fans Choir |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/articles/4yNG1ZWbChv6Vl4DB6NgKwl/songs-of-praise-fa-cup-fans-choir |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=17 April 2015 |archive-date=18 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150418101512/http://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/articles/4yNG1ZWbChv6Vl4DB6NgKwl/songs-of-praise-fa-cup-fans-choir |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Following the semi-finals, both clubs met at Wembley to discuss final arrangements. Arsenal were designated as the "home" team because they were first alphabetically. Despite this, Aston Villa won the pre-match coin toss for choice of strips and chose to wear their home claret and blue [[Kit (association football)|kit]] that they had worn for all of the previous rounds. Arsenal decided to wear their yellow and blue away strip instead of their dark blue cup kit.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arsenal to wear yellow and blue away strip for FA Cup final showdown with Aston Villa |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/apr/24/arsenal-wear-away-kit-fa-cup-final-aston-villa |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=24 April 2015 |access-date=18 May 2015 |archive-date=10 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910153000/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2015/apr/24/arsenal-wear-away-kit-fa-cup-final-aston-villa |url-status=live }}</ref> 
The BBC devoted a whole day of programming to the final, reviving the tradition of its all-day coverage.<ref name=beebcov>{{cite web |title=BBC to bring back all-day coverage of final for 2015 |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/32801726 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=19 May 2015 |access-date=19 May 2015 |archive-date=21 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150521212827/http://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/32801726 |url-status=live }}</ref>  Both Aston Villa and Arsenal received a ticket allocation of 25,000 for the game with 17,000 being offered to members of Club Wembley, de facto season ticket holders at Wembley Stadium. Ticket prices ranged from £50, £70, £90 and £120.<ref>{{cite web |title=FA defends Villa's 'low' FA cup final ticket allocation |url=http://www.itv.com/news/central/update/2015-04-29/fa-defends-villas-low-fa-cup-final-ticket-allocation/ |publisher=[[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] |access-date=29 April 2015 |archive-date=1 May 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150501214719/http://www.itv.com/news/central/update/2015-04-29/fa-defends-villas-low-fa-cup-final-ticket-allocation/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

